    21:11[pjb@thalassa org.xquartz:0 bocl-master 31Gi]$



0- the option must be named --on-error, not -on-error.
   short options start with a single dash: -f
   long options start with two dashes: --foo

1- --ON-ERROR DEBUG must call INVOKE-DEBUGGER upon error.

2- there must not be any such interaction between bocl and the surrounding shell
   (I guess this goes thru escape codes and the terminal? or some other way?
   here the test is in emacs M-x shell)

3- INVOKE-DEBUGGER must implement a "minimal" CL debugger with the following features:

     - display the error message (princ the condition parameter),

     - print the backtrace (obtained from the kernel, see below),

     - print the list of currently available restarts (restart menu),

     - debugging REPL:
        - read a command:
            - if it's a debugger command, execute it,
            - if it's some lisp expression, evaluate it and print the results,
        - and loop

     - an easy way to distinguish debugger commands from lisp expressions is to
       use keywords for debugger commands and restart numbers. eg:

       :backtrace :type call :verbose 1  :count 10
       :backtrace :type binding :verbose 2 :start 20 :count 10
       :backtrace :type all :verbose 0

       :restart 2
       :restart try-again
       :restart abort-connection

       :continue
       :abort
       :quit

       Non-debugger command keywords would evaluate as lisp expressions.

     - debugger commands:
         - select a restart and invoke-restart it.
           (note if the restart is interactive this may lead to some interactive I/O
           to fill the parameters for the restart),
           The restart can be selected by "name" or by number in the restart menu.
         - print the backtrace again (with options, such as range of frames, verbose or not).
         - continue, ie. return NIL from invoke-debugger.
         - abort, ie. non-local exit back to the toplevel REPL.
         - quit, ie. non-local exit from the toplevel REPL, aka QUIT.

     There is no provision to perform stepping or tracing execution
     from the debugger (the user can use CL:TRACE and CL:BREAK).

     And there is no provision yet to inspect or modify the stack
     frames in the backtrace.

     The debugger REPL evaluates the expressions in the dynamic
     context of the function that calls INVOKE-DEBUGGER (or ERROR,
     CERROR, or SIGNAL), but not the lexical context.

     The expressions given at the debugger REPL can modify the state
     of the dynamic variables and of the lisp objects that are bound
     in the lexical context of the function (but not the lexical
     variables).

   So INVOKE-DEBUGGER implements a minimal debugger that can be
   implemented entirely and conformingly in Common Lisp.

   The kernel provide a list of frame from the top-of-the-stack down
   to the bottom (the call ot bocl from the main).
   For now, each frame is an opaque object.

   (bocl:backtrace) --> list-of-frames (first is top-of-the-stack)
   (bocl:frame-kind frame) -> keyword denoting the kind of frame
   (bocl:frame-name frame) -> function name (lambda expression) or special operator or nil
   (bocl:print-frame frame stream :verbosity vlevel)
   verbosity-level = 0 just print the frame kind and name
   verbosity-level = 1 print the parameter names (function) or the variable names (let), without values.
   verbosity-level = 2 print also the values bound to the parameters or the variables, subject to
   the printer variables as bound by INVOKE-DEBUGGER from the bocl:*debugger-print-…* variables.


   | debugger variable                  | default value | Printer variable bound by INVOKE-DEBUGGER |
   |------------------------------------+---------------+-------------------------------------------|
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-ARRAY*        | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-ARRAY*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-BASE*         | 10            | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-BASE*                  |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-CASE*         | :UPCASE       | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-CASE*                  |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-CIRCLE*       | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-CIRCLE*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-ESCAPE*       | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-ESCAPE*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-GENSYM*       | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-GENSYM*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-LENGTH*       | 8             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-LENGTH*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-LEVEL*        | 3             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-LEVEL*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-LINES*        | 2             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-LINES*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-PRETTY*       | NIL           | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-PRETTY*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-RADIX*        | NIL           | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-RADIX*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-READABLY*     | NIL           | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-READABLY*              |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-RIGHT-MARGIN* | 72            | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-RIGHT-MARGIN*          |
   |------------------------------------+---------------+-------------------------------------------|



----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (slice 46, v1.1.3) — KEPT OPEN (large feature)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
DONE:
- Point 0: the option is now named --on-error (two dashes); -on-error kept as a
  legacy alias (sources/main.c).

ALREADY PRESENT:
- Restart system: FIND-RESTART / INVOKE-RESTART / RESTART-CASE / COMPUTE-RESTARTS
  (sources/conditions.c, sources/eval.c, prelude). INVOKE-DEBUGGER and BREAK are
  currently STUBS (prelude: invoke-debugger just (error condition)).

REMAINING (the bulk — a real minimal debugger):
- Point 3: a kernel backtrace API — (bocl:backtrace) -> frames, (bocl:frame-kind),
  (bocl:frame-name), (bocl:print-frame frame stream :verbosity v). Requires C
  support to expose the call/binding stack as frame objects.
- A real INVOKE-DEBUGGER: print condition + backtrace + restart menu, then a
  debugger REPL with keyword commands (:backtrace, :restart N/name, :continue,
  :abort, :quit) and lisp-expression evaluation in the error's dynamic context.
- The 12 BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-* variables (table in this issue) bound around the
  CL:*PRINT-* variables by INVOKE-DEBUGGER.
- Point 1: wire --on-error debug to call INVOKE-DEBUGGER on an unhandled error.
- Point 2: investigate the emacs `M-x shell` input-pushback (bocl appears to exit
  and the typed line reaches bash) — likely terminal/EOF handling, reproduce
  under M-x shell.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (slice 51, v1.1.8) — the minimal debugger is implemented.
----------------------------------------------------------------------
DONE:
- Point 1: --on-error debug now calls INVOKE-DEBUGGER on an unhandled error.
  The launcher publishes the action as BOCL:*ON-ERROR* (:debug/:exit/:abort/
  :appease, sources/main.c) and the REPL routes every form through the new
  %REPL-EVAL wrapper (sources/bocl.c + prelude) which, on an unhandled
  SERIOUS-CONDITION, enters the debugger (:debug), reports and returns to the
  prompt (:appease/:abort), or reports and exits (:exit).
- Point 2: the REPL no longer dies on an unhandled error — %REPL-EVAL wraps each
  form in RESTART-CASE (ABORT) + HANDLER-BIND, so an error returns to the prompt
  instead of letting the C error path exit() the process (which is what dropped
  the typed-ahead line back to the surrounding bash / M-x shell).  Verified: an
  error under --on-error debug/appease leaves the REPL alive and later forms run.
- Point 3 — kernel backtrace API: new C primitive %BACKTRACE (sources/conditions.c)
  walks the dynamic control-frame stack; Lisp wrappers BOCL:BACKTRACE,
  BOCL:FRAME-KIND, BOCL:FRAME-NAME, BOCL:PRINT-FRAME (frame stream :verbosity).
- Point 3 — INVOKE-DEBUGGER (prelude, replacing the old (error condition) stub):
  honours *DEBUGGER-HOOK*; binds the CL *PRINT-* specials from the 13
  BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-* variables (all defined); prints the condition, the
  backtrace and the restart menu; runs a debugger REPL on *DEBUG-IO* (falling
  back to *STANDARD-INPUT*/*STANDARD-OUTPUT*, since *DEBUG-IO*/*TERMINAL-IO* are
  NIL in bocl) with keyword commands :BACKTRACE :RESTARTS :RESTART(N|name)
  :CONTINUE :ABORT :QUIT :HELP, a bare integer to pick a restart by number, and
  any other form evaluated as Lisp in the error's dynamic context.
- BREAK now enters the debugger with a CONTINUE restart (was a stub).
Verified end to end via piped stdin: error -> debugger banner + backtrace +
restart menu; :backtrace / :restarts re-print; :restart name and bare number
invoke restarts; lisp forms evaluate (e.g. *print-base* shows the debugger
binding 10); :abort/:continue return to the REPL; break + :continue resumes;
handled errors (handler-case) do NOT enter the debugger.

PROGRESS (slice 46/51) REMAINING (kept open — needs a kernel function-call stack):
- The backtrace currently exposes the active dynamic CONTROL frames (block,
  catch, handler-case/bind, restart, unwind-protect, dynamic-binding, tagbody),
  NOT function-call frames.  So FRAME-NAME gives function names only for BLOCK
  frames (a DEFUN body is a block named after the function) and PRINT-FRAME
  :verbosity 1/2 (parameter names / bound values) is not yet meaningful.  A real
  call/binding stack in the kernel (each apply pushing a frame, restored across
  longjmp) is the prerequisite; it is the larger remaining piece of this issue.
- Cosmetic: the debugger's own frames (BACKTRACE/%PRINT-BACKTRACE/%RUN-DEBUGGER)
  appear at the top of the backtrace; they could be elided.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (slice 114, v1.3.1) — CLOSED: function-call frames + cosmetics done.
----------------------------------------------------------------------
DONE — the remaining pieces above are now implemented:
- Function-call frames.  The implicit block APPLY pushes around a function body
  (eval_body_in_block) now records the function object and its parameter
  environment in the block frame (sources/kernel_types.h Frame.parameters.block;
  set in sources/eval.c; explicit BLOCK forms set both to NIL).  %BACKTRACE
  (sources/conditions.c) turns such a frame into a (:CALL name (param . value)...)
  descriptor, computing the parameter/value alist on demand via the new
  environment_local_bindings() (sources/environment.c / .h).
- BOCL:FRAME-KIND now returns :CALL for call frames; BOCL:FRAME-NAME returns the
  function name; new BOCL:FRAME-BINDINGS returns the (param . value) alist;
  BOCL:PRINT-FRAME :verbosity 1 prints parameter names and :verbosity 2 prints
  names=values (subject to the *DEBUGGER-PRINT-* bindings).  Verified:
  (defun foo (a b) (bocl:backtrace)) (foo 42 'hi) -> a :CALL frame (FOO A=42 B=HI).
- Debugger backtrace cosmetics: %DEBUGGER-FRAMES elides every frame up to and
  including the INVOKE-DEBUGGER frame, so the trace starts at the error's own call
  chain (CRASH/OUTER with their argument values), not the debugger machinery.
- The debugger REPL is now line-oriented (reads a whole line, parses tokens), so
  the issue's command syntax works: ":backtrace :type call :verbose 2 :count 10",
  ":restart try-again", ":restart 2", bare integer, :continue/:abort/:quit/:help,
  and any other form(s) evaluate as Lisp.  %PRINT-BACKTRACE honours
  :verbosity/:type/:start/:count.
Verified end to end (--on-error debug, piped stdin): error -> banner + elided
backtrace (verbosity 1) + restart menu; :backtrace :type call :verbose 2 shows
the user frames with argument values; debugger Lisp eval works ((* 6 7) -> 42);
:abort returns to a live top-level REPL.  No regressions across the must-* suite
(must-condition 147/0, -loop 852/0, -cons 728/0, -sequence 3851/0, -do 75/0,
-symbol 113/0, -package 335/0).

SPEC: the full debugger specification (UI + API with the CL implementation) is in
specifications/debugger-specifications.org.

